    note: no CODEOWNERS file; protect .numinance/policy/** with branch protection and a CODEOWNERS rule so agents cannot change their own gates
=== .numinance/README.md ===
# .numinance

Written by `numi init`. Typed Numinance configuration for this repository (design section 8). Review it like code.

- `policy/` is the protected location: a document there carries authority only when it is read from the protected
  branch of the repository, so protect `.numinance/policy/**` with branch protection and CODEOWNERS. Agents cannot change
  their own gates.
- Toolchain: Python 3.11, package manager poetry, verifiers lint, typecheck.
- CI: github-actions and jenkins.
- Discovery: `DISCOVERY.md` lists what `numi init` found in this repository and why it chose each value above.
- Ownership: no CODEOWNERS file was found.
- Autonomy: PR only. The ladder level is `AUTONOMY_LEVEL_PR`: the agent opens pull requests and never merges.
  `PR_ONLY` is a separate thing, a state of the engine's breaker.
- The organization documents (stage registry, grants, floors) were written to the organization policy repository
  you named with `--org-dir`. Validate with `numi validate --org THAT_DIR .` (engine build).

The kernel floors (separation of duties, plan before side effects, evidence by the runner only, autonomy and residency
ceilings and others) are part of the Numinance engine, always on, and no document in this tree or in an organization
repository can weaken them. Nothing here references them, because there is nothing to reference.

Jenkins: the ciexecutor-jenkins provider exists in replay mode only (a live run is owed; not yet in the release bundle); see the comments in `policy/sdlc-profile.yaml`.
The repository profile binds GitHub Actions. The Jenkins binding is only a grant (an org AdapterSelector) or an inert
document, and a one-line edit of the profile could rebind to the Jenkins provider. That rebinding is the operator's
explicit edit, and `numi validate` does not check that a provider is installed, so it would pass and still run nothing.
=== .numinance/policy/sdlc-profile.yaml ===
# SDLCProfile of this repository, written by "numi init". It binds the names defined in this tree
# (or granted by the organization policy repository) to this repository.
#
# CI: github-actions and jenkins
#   github-actions: the ciexecutor provider dev.numinance.ci-github-actions exists in the open plugins (replay and live builds).
#   It starts a workflow_dispatch workflow that the repository already defines on its default branch
#   (the README of that provider says what the workflow must declare). A live run is not yet verified.
#   jenkins: the ciexecutor provider dev.numinance.ciexecutor-jenkins exists in the open plugins in replay mode only: a live run
#   is owed and it is not yet in the release bundle. Validation does not check that a provider is installed.
#
# The adapter schema binds ONE ciexecutor per scope, so with both CI systems selected the binding below is the one that
# takes effect: github-actions, the only CI provider that exists.
# The organization policy repository also grants the Jenkins binding (AdapterSelector ci-jenkins), so a repository can
# switch to it by editing the ciexecutor line below; two ciexecutor bindings at once are not possible yet.
apiVersion: numinance.dev/v1
kind: SDLCProfile
metadata: {name: python-repo, scope: SCOPE_REPO}
spec:
  playbooks: [bugfix-bounded]
  toolchains: [python-3.11]
  adapters:
    scm: {provider: dev.numinance.scm-github, instance: main}
    ciexecutor: {provider: dev.numinance.ci-github-actions, instance: ci}
##### organization policy repository
=== .numinance/README.md ===
# .numinance (organization policy repository)

Written by `numi init --org-dir`. Org-scoped documents: the stage registry `pr-only`, the playbook and toolchain
definitions, the grants of an SDLCProfile and the autonomy floors (a ceiling of PR for every change, SUGGEST for HIGH
risk). Only the org scope sets these. Protect `.numinance/policy/**` on the default branch. Read it with
`numi validate --org THIS_DIR REPOSITORY` (engine build).

The kernel floors of the Numinance engine are always on and no document here can weaken them.
=== .numinance/policy/autonomy.yaml ===
# AutonomyPolicy "pr-only", written by "numi init". The ladder level is AUTONOMY_LEVEL_PR: the agent may open pull requests
# and may never merge. (PR_ONLY is a breaker state of the engine, not a ladder level; this document is the ladder side.)
apiVersion: numinance.dev/v1
kind: AutonomyPolicy
metadata: {name: pr-only, scope: SCOPE_ORG}
spec:
  defaults: {level: AUTONOMY_LEVEL_PR}
  # Floors are a ceiling and only the org scope may set them. A floor matches the risk class it names and every higher
  # one, and a match may not be empty, so risk READ (the lowest class) makes the PR cap apply to every change.
  # HIGH risk stays at SUGGEST, the engine's own shipped floor for it.
  floors:
    rules:
      - {match: {risk: RISK_CLASS_HIGH}, max_level: AUTONOMY_LEVEL_SUGGEST}
      - {match: {risk: RISK_CLASS_READ}, max_level: AUTONOMY_LEVEL_PR}
=== .numinance/policy/ci-jenkins.yaml ===
# Written by "numi init --org-dir" when both CI systems are selected. An AdapterSelector of the org scope grants the
# Jenkins binding: a repository may select it as its ciexecutor. It is a grant only and is not bound today: the profiles
# (sdlc-profile.yaml of the org and of the repository) bind GitHub Actions and are applied after this one, because one
# ciexecutor binding is possible per scope. A repository could rebind to this binding by a one-line edit of its profile;
# that is the operator's explicit edit, and validate does not check that a provider is installed.
#
# The provider dev.numinance.ciexecutor-jenkins exists in replay mode only: a live run is owed and it is not yet in the
# release bundle. An on-prem Jenkins server needs an egress allow-list entry for its host and a credential handle from the broker.
apiVersion: numinance.dev/v1
kind: AdapterSelector
metadata: {name: ci-jenkins, scope: SCOPE_ORG}
spec:
  ports:
    ciexecutor: {provider: dev.numinance.ciexecutor-jenkins, instance: jenkins-onprem}
=== .numinance/policy/playbook-bugfix-bounded.yaml ===
# Playbook "bugfix-bounded", written by "numi init": a bounded bug fix that ends in a pull request. The verifier names are
# the ones of the LanguageToolchain python-3.11.
apiVersion: numinance.dev/v1
kind: Playbook
metadata: {name: bugfix-bounded, scope: SCOPE_ORG}
spec:
  change_type: bugfix
  steps:
    # One step per identity kind. The capability_set names are the ones the deployment defines: implement (author-agent:
    # the branch push and the pull request), review (reviewer-agent: verifier.run), release (release-pipeline: the read-only
    # status of the pull request). No step merges: Numinance opens pull requests only.
    - {name: fix, capability_set: implement, risk: RISK_CLASS_MEDIUM, evidence: [diff, pr-ref], verifiers: [lint, typecheck]}
    - {name: review, capability_set: review, risk: RISK_CLASS_LOW}
    - {name: pr-status, capability_set: release, risk: RISK_CLASS_LOW}
  max_repair_attempts: 2
  qualification: {min_runs: 20, max_rework_rate: 1000, max_regression_rate: 0, max_rollback_rate: 50, zero_security_escapes: true}
=== .numinance/policy/sdlc-profile.yaml ===
# SDLCProfile of the organization policy repository, written by "numi init --org-dir". Only the org scope selects the
# stage registry and grants playbook and toolchain names to repositories; a repository profile that asks for a name that
# is not granted here gets it recorded as "outside the org-granted set" and not applied. The same holds for an adapter
# binding: a repository may select only a binding (port, provider, instance) that an org document binds.
#
# CI: github-actions and jenkins. The ciexecutor binding below is the one of ci-github-actions.
# Jenkins: dev.numinance.ciexecutor-jenkins exists in replay mode only (a live run is owed; not yet in the release bundle).
apiVersion: numinance.dev/v1
kind: SDLCProfile
metadata: {name: org-baseline, scope: SCOPE_ORG}
spec:
  stages: pr-only
  playbooks: [bugfix-bounded]
  toolchains: [python-3.11]
  adapters:
    scm: {provider: dev.numinance.scm-github, instance: main}
    ciexecutor: {provider: dev.numinance.ci-github-actions, instance: ci}
=== .numinance/policy/stage-registry.yaml ===
# StageRegistry "pr-only", written by "numi init": the stage names of this repository mapped onto the fixed Change state
# machine. The registry names no stage for the Merging state, so no stage of it can merge; the last stage is the human
# pr-gate. Each CI system has its own verify stage. Only the org scope selects a registry
# (SDLCProfile.stages), so below the org this registry is defined but not selected.
apiVersion: numinance.dev/v1
kind: StageRegistry
metadata: {name: pr-only, scope: SCOPE_ORG}
spec:
  stages:
    - {name: triage, maps_to: CHANGE_STATE_CLASSIFIED}
    - {name: plan-review, maps_to: CHANGE_STATE_PLAN_GATE, gate: {kind: GATE_KIND_PLAN}, requires: [triage]}
    - {name: implement, maps_to: CHANGE_STATE_IMPLEMENTING, requires: [plan-review]}
    # Static checks run by the verifier provider on the candidate.
    - {name: verify-static, maps_to: CHANGE_STATE_VERIFYING, requires: [implement]}
    # GitHub Actions: runs the repository's own workflow through the ciexecutor port.
    - {name: ci-github-actions, maps_to: CHANGE_STATE_VERIFYING, requires: [verify-static]}
    # Jenkins: the ciexecutor-jenkins provider exists in replay mode (a live run is owed; not yet in the release bundle).
    - {name: ci-jenkins, maps_to: CHANGE_STATE_VERIFYING, requires: [verify-static]}
    - name: pr-gate
      maps_to: CHANGE_STATE_RELEASE_GATE
      gate:
        kind: GATE_KIND_HUMAN
        approver_role: maintainer
        sod: {distinct_from: [author-agent, reviewer-agent]}
        required_approvals: 1
      requires: [ci-github-actions, ci-jenkins]
=== .numinance/policy/toolchain-python.yaml ===
# LanguageToolchain for Python, written by "numi init". The toolchain is explicit: language, version, package manager and
# the verifier set are the values chosen by the flags of "numi init" or detected in the repository; edit them here.
# The verifier provider takes the check to run in "config.check". It may also take "settings", the text of the tool's own
# settings file (ruff.toml, mypy.ini, pytest.ini); it never reads settings files of the candidate.
apiVersion: numinance.dev/v1
kind: LanguageToolchain
metadata: {name: python-3.11, scope: SCOPE_ORG}
spec:
  language: python
  version: "3.11"
  verifiers:
    - {name: lint, provider: dev.numinance.verifier-python, config: {check: ruff, timeout_seconds: 60}}
    - {name: typecheck, provider: dev.numinance.verifier-python, config: {check: mypy, timeout_seconds: 120}}
  package_manager: poetry
